# Aleph — local stack configuration.
#
#   cp deploy/compose/.env.example deploy/compose/.env
#
# Then set ONE thing: where your models live (§1). Everything else already
# works for a local run. Secrets marked CHANGE-ME are fine for localhost and
# must be replaced before this is reachable by anyone else.
#
# `apps/api/tests/unit/test_env_settings_reconciled.py` fails if a required
# settings field is missing here, or if an ALEPH_* key here has no backing
# field — so this file cannot silently drift from the code.

# =============================================================================
# 1. YOUR MODEL ENDPOINT — the only thing you must set
# =============================================================================
# Aleph serves no models. It connects out to any OpenAI-compatible endpoint:
# LiteLLM, Ollama, vLLM, OpenRouter, Bedrock behind a proxy, or a hosted API.
#
# `host.docker.internal` reaches YOUR machine from inside the containers, and
# compose maps it on Linux too (see `extra_hosts` in docker-compose.yml), so the
# same line works on macOS, Linux and against a remote host.
#
#   LiteLLM proxy on this machine   http://host.docker.internal:4000
#   Ollama on this machine          http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1
#   vLLM on this machine            http://host.docker.internal:8000/v1
#   somewhere else entirely         https://models.example.com/v1
LITELLM_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:4000
INSIGHTS_LITELLM_API_KEY=CHANGE-ME-model-endpoint-key

# Your own rates, for models your gateway does not price.
#
# Aleph ships no price list. It reads what the gateway reports — preferring
# `/model/info`, which carries exact rates — and falls back to `/v1/models`,
# which carries ids only. That fallback is the NORMAL case: a virtual key
# restricted to `llm_api_routes` cannot read `/model/info` at all.
#
# Anything the gateway does not price is recorded as `pricing_source=unknown`
# and costed at zero, which is honest and unhelpful. Point this at a JSON file
# of your own rates and those models are costed and labelled `static` —
# asserted by you rather than reported by the endpoint. Never overrides a rate
# the gateway did report.
#
# Format: {"models": {"<model-id-or-prefix*>": {"input_per_token": "0.000003",
# "output_per_token": "0.000015"}}}. Unset uses the shipped defaults at
# packages/aleph-models/src/aleph_models/model_hints.json.
#ALEPH_MODEL_HINTS_PATH=/path/to/your/model_hints.json

# =============================================================================
# 2. Secrets — generated for you by scripts/bootstrap-local.sh
# =============================================================================
# Signs the short-lived tokens workers use to call back into the API.
# This does ONE job. It used to do three — it also encrypted every stored
# connector credential — which meant the correct response to a leaked signing
# key (rotate it) destroyed every third-party API key and OAuth grant in the
# deployment, permanently and without warning.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
ALEPH_AGENT_TOKEN_SECRET=CHANGE-ME-run-openssl-rand-hex-32

# Encrypts every stored connector credential. A DIFFERENT value from the one
# above — using the same string for both re-creates exactly the coupling that
# was removed. Required; the API and workers refuse to start without it, and it
# is not padded, so anything under 32 bytes is rejected rather than stretched.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
ALEPH_CREDENTIAL_MASTER_KEY=CHANGE-ME-run-openssl-rand-hex-32

# Only for a deployment that already stored credentials before the keys were
# split, or that has rotated ALEPH_CREDENTIAL_MASTER_KEY and has not finished
# re-encrypting. Holds the PREVIOUS key. Leave unset on a fresh install; unset
# means "old rows were keyed by ALEPH_AGENT_TOKEN_SECRET", which is what they
# were. Remove it once `python -m aleph_connectors.reencrypt --dry-run` reports
# zero rows — the full procedure is in docs/operations.md, "Rotating a secret".
#ALEPH_CREDENTIAL_LEGACY_KEY=

# Langfuse's own secrets. These used to be three more jobs for
# ALEPH_AGENT_TOKEN_SECRET (docker-compose.yml passes it as NEXTAUTH_SECRET,
# SALT and ENCRYPTION_KEY), which put Aleph's agent-signing key inside a
# third-party container's session cookies and database encryption.
# Generate each: openssl rand -hex 32
LANGFUSE_NEXTAUTH_SECRET=CHANGE-ME-run-openssl-rand-hex-32
LANGFUSE_SALT=CHANGE-ME-run-openssl-rand-hex-32
LANGFUSE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=CHANGE-ME-run-openssl-rand-hex-32

# Postgres superuser password. Compose builds DATABASE_URL from it.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE-ME-postgres

# =============================================================================
# 3. Backing services — compose fills these in; override only to point elsewhere
# =============================================================================
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://aleph:CHANGE-ME-postgres@postgres:5432/aleph
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0

# Where workers re-enter the API over HTTP with a minted agent token.
ALEPH_API_INTERNAL_URL=http://api:8000

# The URL the browser uses. Change if you serve Aleph on another host or port.
ALEPH_SELF_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Origins the API accepts browser requests from. Compose derives this from
# WEB_PORT; set it explicitly only when serving the UI from a LAN or tailnet
# address. `allow_credentials` forbids "*", so this must be an explicit list.
ALEPH_CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173

# =============================================================================
# 4. Tracing — off unless you run `--profile tracing`
# =============================================================================
# These stay set even with the profile off: the settings models require them,
# and with nothing listening the exporters simply fail to deliver. Turning the
# profile on starts Langfuse and the collector at exactly these addresses.
# Tracing is opt-in and lives behind `--profile tracing`. Leave this EMPTY
# unless that profile is up: a configured endpoint with no collector behind
# it makes every process retry forever and buries real errors in the logs.
#   docker compose --profile tracing up -d
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4317
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
LANGFUSE_HOST=http://langfuse:3000
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk-lf-CHANGE-ME
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=sk-lf-CHANGE-ME

# =============================================================================
# 5. Behaviour — sensible defaults, change if you want to
# =============================================================================
ALEPH_ENV=local

# Aleph runs single-user; `local` is the only mode (docs/decisions.md D6).
ALEPH_AUTH_MODE=local
ALEPH_AUTH_CLIENT_ID=

# Which capability→model bindings a new project starts from.
ALEPH_DEFAULT_MODEL_PROFILE=aleph-dev

# Assets live on the local filesystem by default. `--profile s3` starts MinIO;
# switch this to `s3` and fill in §6 to use it.
ALEPH_ASSET_BACKEND=fs
ALEPH_ASSET_ROOT=data/assets

# Crossref and OpenAlex ask for a contact address and give better rate limits
# when you supply a real one.
ALEPH_SCHOLAR_MAILTO=dev@aleph.local

# =============================================================================
# 6. Object store — only when ALEPH_ASSET_BACKEND=s3 (`--profile s3`)
# =============================================================================
ALEPH_S3_ENDPOINT=minio:9000
ALEPH_S3_ACCESS_KEY=aleph
ALEPH_S3_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE-ME-minio
ALEPH_S3_BUCKET=aleph-assets
ALEPH_S3_SECURE=false

# =============================================================================
# 7. Host ports — change any that clash with something already running
# =============================================================================
# A conflict here does not look like a conflict. If another process already
# holds the port on 127.0.0.1 (a stale VS Code forward, a local Postgres, a
# tunnel), Docker still binds 0.0.0.0 and starts happily — connections then
# reach the OTHER listener, which accepts the TCP handshake and never speaks.
# You get a hang, not "address in use". Change the number here rather than
# hunting the process.
#
#   lsof -nP -iTCP:5432 -sTCP:LISTEN     # who already has it
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
REDIS_PORT=6379
API_PORT_HOST=8000
RUNTIME_PORT=4000
WEB_PORT=5173
LANGFUSE_PORT=3000

# Only published by the opt-in profiles, and left here because these three were
# the last hardcoded ports in the compose file — on a machine that already runs
# something on 9000, `--profile s3` failed to start with no way to move it.
OTEL_PORT=4317
MINIO_PORT=9000
MINIO_CONSOLE_PORT=9001

# The sandbox's queue MUST be a different Redis from the platform bus, or the
# kernel refuses to boot: code-runner would otherwise share a channel with agent
# tokens and privileged queues. Compose provides `runner-redis` for this.
CODE_RUNNER_REDIS_URL=redis://runner-redis:6379/0

# =============================================================================
# 8. Memory caps — the defaults are measured; raise them, do not lower them
# =============================================================================
# Each of these sets mem_limit AND memswap_limit to the same value, which is the
# only way compose can say "no swap" (`deploy.resources` cannot express it).
#
# A cap BELOW a service's working set is worse than no cap, and the restart
# policy hides it: the container is OOM-killed, restarted, and crash-loops while
# `docker compose ps` reports "Up 1 second (health: starting)" forever. That is
# not hypothetical — `web` was first set to 1g and did exactly this.
#
# Measured at idle on a working stack, against the shipped defaults:
#
#   api              210 MiB    default 2g   reads uploads into memory
#   workers          130 MiB    default 2g   normalizes whole documents
#   web             1.07 GiB    default 3g   Vite dev server + esbuild
#   copilot-runtime  122 MiB    default 512m a thin SSE proxy
#
# Uncomment to override. postgres and redis are deliberately uncapped — Postgres
# manages its own memory through shared_buffers/work_mem, and a cgroup limit
# under those OOM-kills mid-query instead of applying backpressure.
#API_MEM_LIMIT=2g
#WORKERS_MEM_LIMIT=2g
#WEB_MEM_LIMIT=3g
#RUNTIME_MEM_LIMIT=512m

# How often the gateway's price list is re-read, in seconds (default 900).
# Pricing used to be discovered exactly once, at boot: a gateway that came up
# LATER was never priced, so every call it served was recorded
# `pricing_source="unknown"` with `cost_usd=0` forever.
# ALEPH_GATEWAY_PRICING_REFRESH_S=900

# How soon to retry after a failed refresh, in seconds (default 30). Shorter
# than the interval on purpose — a gateway that is briefly unreachable should
# not go unpriced for a quarter of an hour.
# ALEPH_GATEWAY_PRICING_RETRY_S=30
